## Project Pipeline

The notebook runs as a **shared setup spine** followed by **two independent model tracks**
that converge for comparison and final evaluation. The tracks must run in **separate kernel
sessions**: TensorFlow does not release GPU memory while its objects remain in the kernel,
so running both in one session causes a CUDA out-of-memory error in whichever runs second.

```text
  1. Setup                    imports, constants, kagglehub download
        |
  2. Load and split           validate files, record metadata, stratified 70/15/15   
        |
  3. Preprocessor and fit     MidiPreprocessor, target length from metadata          (instant)
        |
  4. Feature engineering      build or restore the window store                      (cached)
        |
  = = = = = = = = = =  restart runtime between tracks  = = = = = = = = = =
        |
        +---------------------------+---------------------------+
        |                                                       |
  5a. LSTM track (Keras)                                  5b. CNN track (PyTorch)
      BUILD_LSTM_VIEW = True                                  BUILD_LSTM_VIEW = False
      guard -> architecture                                   loader -> architecture
      -> train -> evaluate                                    -> helpers -> train -> evaluate
        |                                                       |
      back up .keras + registry to Drive                      back up .pt + registry to Drive
        |                                                       |
        +---------------------------+---------------------------+
                                    |
                          6. Compare and select    merge registry, rank dev piece macro-F1
                                    |
                          7. Test once and package  selected model only, then artifacts
```

**Three constraints that matter when re-running:**

1. `BUILD_LSTM_VIEW` flips per track — `True` materializes the time-pooled sequence view the
   LSTM needs; `False` skips it for the CNN, whose PyTorch `Dataset` reads the raw memmaps
   directly and would otherwise waste ~9 GB of RAM.
2. In the CNN track the **evaluation helpers cell must run before training**, because
   `train_cnn` calls `predict_probs` and `piece_level` each epoch to compute the dev
   piece-level macro-F1 used for checkpoint selection.
3. Both tracks write to the same on-disk results registry, and Colab's `/content` is wiped
   on VM recycle — **back up the checkpoint and registry to Drive after each track.**

**After a disconnect:** re-run the spine (steps 1–4) plus only the cells for the track in
progress. Step 4 restores from the fingerprinted cache in seconds, and evaluation cells
reload weights from disk, so no retraining is needed unless the VM was recycled and the
checkpoints were lost. Every downstream cell carries a prerequisite guard naming any missing
variables and the cells that produce them.
